## Why This Is Asked
Tests environment isolation via provider aliases and per-env backends, plus CI gating for safe promotion to QA.

## Key Concepts
- Provider alias isolation
- Backend per environment
- CI gated plan/apply workflow

## Code Example
```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.4"

  backend "s3" {
    key    = "envs/qa/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-west-2"
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  alias = "qa"
  region = "us-west-2"
  assume_role {
    role_arn = "arn:aws:iam::ACCOUNT_QA:role/TerraformRole"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle secrets across environments?
- What are trade-offs of provider alias vs separate workspaces?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of remote state isolation, cross-env data sharing, and basic CI gating for beginner Terraform workflows.

## Key Concepts
- terraform_remote_state data source
- backend alias and per-env state keys
- var-files for environment specificity (qa.tfvars)
- CI gating with plan validation before apply

## Code Example
```javascript
// qa backend example (HCL in Terraform syntax, shown as JS for formatting)
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket         = "my-terraform-state"
    key            = "envs/qa/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "us-east-1"
    dynamodb_table = "tf-locks"
  }
}

data "terraform_remote_state" "prod" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "my-terraform-state"
    key    = "envs/prod/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to multi-region VPCs?
- How would you test for drift between prod and qa after apply?
